@prefix : <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto#> .
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> .
@prefix cco: <https://www.w3id.org/cco/cco#> .
@prefix gro: <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xml: <http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix data: <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/data#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix gro-au: <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/au#> .
@prefix gro-ca: <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/ca#> .
@prefix gro-uk: <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/uk#> .
@prefix gro-us: <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/us#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@base <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto#> .

<https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                 owl:versionIRI <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/v1.0> ;
                                 dc:creator "Umair Arshad" ;
                                 dc:title "Unified legislation regulatory ontology"@en ;
                                 dcterms:abstract "Ontology engineering is a complex, time-consuming, and error-prone process, and is particularly demanding in regulatory domains, where knowledge is expressed through obligations, permissions, prohibitions, conditions, and exceptions that require disciplined representation. Existing LLM-based extraction approaches improve scalability, but often struggle to maintain structural consistency in the generated ontology. In this paper, we present a schema- and pattern-guided approach for regulatory ontology synthesis, in which LLM extraction is constrained directly within a target ontology rather than mapped to it afterward. The method incorporates human expertise through iterative prompt refinement, SHACL shape authoring, and repair-function design. It operates in two steps: structured decomposition of source text into typed components, followed by ontology generation guided by (i) a foun- dational schema that provides class and property whitelists, and (ii) on- tologydesignpatternsretrievedthroughembeddingsimilaritytosupport structural alignment. A layered validation architecture combines SHACL conformance checking with SHACL-informed deterministic repair to address property-domain and type-discipline errors introduced during gen- eration. We evaluate the approach through a multi-stage framework covering quality metrics, ontology correctness, and source-text faithfulness. The method is demonstrated on education funding regulations across four jurisdictions, showing that schema- and pattern-constrained generation supports the synthesis of structurally consistent regulatory ontologies." ;
                                 dcterms:contributor "David Corsar" ,
                                                     "Ikechukwu Nkisi-Orji" ;
                                 dcterms:issued "2026-05-23" ;
                                 dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
                                 dcterms:rights "CC-BY 4.0"@en ;
                                 dcterms:title "Synthesising Regulatory Ontologies: A Schema and Pattern-Guided Approach" ;
                                 owl:versionInfo "1.0" .

